// A fallen fruit, lying on the ground. What a ripe `fruit` cell becomes the
// moment it lets go of the plant -- and, per the owner's call of 2026-08-23,
// **the seed rides down inside it**: a windfall cell is an organism-owned
// `CellType::Seed` wearing this material instead of `seed`'s brown, so it
// falls, rolls, piles in hollows and germinates where it comes to rest,
// through the CA rules that already exist and with no fruit-specific
// movement code anywhere.
//
// **Why not just reuse `seed`.** Two reasons, one physical and one that is
// the point of the whole phase. Physically a fallen fruit is heavier and
// far softer than a dry husk: it rots in days where a seed waits seasons,
// it is worth a great deal more to eat, and it slumps to a flat drift where
// seeds interlock and sit. And visually: a `seed`-brown cell dropping out of
// a red crown is the fifth label change in a row that fires, counts, and
// changes nothing anyone can see. The fall is meant to be the satisfying
// object -- `Reports/plant-morphology-reach-2026-08-23.md` §7's own argument
// for carrying the seed rather than converting in place.
(
    name: "windfall",
    display: "Windfall",
    kind: Powder,
    // Heavier than `seed` (0.6) and than water: a dropped fruit sinks. It
    // is
    // the densest thing a plant sheds.
    density: 1.05,
    // **Shallow, unlike `seed`'s 55 and `litter`'s 42.** Fruit is round and
    // slick and it rolls -- which is dispersal doing its job on a slope,
    // and
    // is why a windfall ends up somewhere its parent's shade does not
    // reach.
    friction_angle: 26.0,
    // The ripe palette of `fruit.ron`, one shade darker and duller across
    // the board: this is the same object bruised and off the plant.
    colors: [
        (160, 40, 34),
        (120, 26, 36),
        (84, 42, 94),
        (40, 38, 64),
        (182, 92, 30),
        (176, 146, 48),
    ],
    // Wet flesh. Nearly as hard to light as the fruit it fell from.
    flammability: 0.2,
    burn_temperature: 470.0,
    burn_duration: 40,
    burns_into: "ash",
    heat_conductivity: 0.2,
    // **Rots fast and leaves almost nothing**, on `decay.rs`'s
    // moisture-gated
    // schedule -- the same channel litter uses. Faster than litter in both
    // arms: soft wet flesh is the quickest thing on a forest floor to
    // disappear, where a dry leaf can sit out a season.
    //
    // `decay_yield` matches `litter`'s 0.05 for the reason that file argues
    // at length: the great majority of rotting mass leaves as gas this
    // engine
    // cannot represent, and a 1:1 conversion is a source with no sink.
    decays_into: "soil",
    decay_yield: 0.05,
    decay_chance_damp: 0.7,
    decay_chance_dry: 0.15,
    // **The gnome walks through a drift of them**, like litter. Fallen
    // fruit
    // under a tree is ankle-deep at worst and should not read as quicksand.
    insubstantial: true,
    // **And it falls past a branch rather than sitting on it** -- the
    // 2D-slice
    // rule `MaterialDef::falls_through_organisms` states as data. A fruit
    // that
    // lets go inside a crown is the same case as a shed leaf: the world is
    // one
    // vertical slice of a 3D plant, so a branch one cell wide is not a
    // shelf
    // spanning the wood's whole depth, and going past it is the common
    // outcome.
    //
    // **Its effect here is unmeasured, and saying so is the point.** The
    // census that prompted this line read "2 of 2 standing windfall cells
    // resting on plant tissue" and looked decisive. It was a true number
    // about the wrong question, and the flag's own doc names the trap:
    // *resting on plant tissue* counts both a fruit lodged in a crown and a
    // fruit sitting on a prostrate stem at ground level, and those are
    // opposite verdicts. Neither authored fruiting species has a crown to
    // lodge in -- one is a bare stalk, the other a knee-high thicket -- so
    // almost every cell the metric flagged was the harmless case. With the
    // flag on, the discriminating column (windfall more than 16 rows above
    // the ground) reads 1 and 1 against 1 and 0 without it: nothing.
    //
    // Kept anyway, because it is a claim about what a fruit *is* rather
    // than
    // a tuning knob, and it costs one branch paid only by a windfall
    // sitting
    // on living tissue. It will matter the first time something tall
    // fruits,
    // and that is the run that should measure it.
    //
    // **Note the density argument runs the other way and is not the
    // criterion.** This is 1.05 against `deadwood`'s 0.7, and deadwood
    // deliberately *does* hang up in a crown. That flag's own doc says why
    // geometry cannot decide it: a snapped branch is chunky and a fruit is
    // small and round, and no number already on the material expresses
    // that.
    // The mechanic seconds it -- a windfall carries a live seed, so one
    // lodged
    // mid-canopy germinates mid-canopy, which is the rooted-above-ground
    // failure `plant_probe` already counts.
    falls_through_organisms: true,
    // **No `breaks_into`.** A windfall is already the broken-down form;
    // pointing it at itself would make a fragment loop, which is the same
    // call `litter.ron` makes for the same reason.

    // Worth what it was worth on the branch: nothing about hitting the
    // ground makes a fruit more or less nutritious. **`ant.ron`'s `food:`
    // list is the other half of this** -- `adjacent_food` tests menu
    // membership and nothing else, so a value here with no menu entry is a
    // channel with a writer and no reader, which is the trap `litter.ron`
    // records having shipped once already.
    food_energy: 960.0,
    // In MPa like every other value of this field. Dead, half-rotted tissue
    // -- the softest thing in the world, under `snow`'s 0.2.
    penetration_resistance: 0.05,
    food_class: -1.0,
)
